# Language server rules

## ESM import compatibility (Cursor runtime)

Cursor may run strict Node ESM runtimes. Prefer explicit `.js` import suffixes where required:
- `vscode-languageserver/node.js`
- `antlr4ts/tree/ParseTreeWalker.js`
- `antlr4ts/ParserRuleContext.js`
- `antlr4ts/Token.js`

## ParseTreeWalker wrapping safety

- Bind original walker methods before replacing/wrapping them.
- Skip terminal nodes that do not have `ruleContext`.
- Guard parser/token/walker code paths against stack overflow and degrade gracefully where possible.

## VXML usage + unused symbol indexing

- Functions/units referenced from VXML are counted during `rebuildUsageIndexes()` (actions, `init`, `destroy`, `function` bindings, etc.).
- **Unused `.lang` keys** (`helium-dsl-unused`): `diagnostics.unused.languageEntries` — default **Info**, `"None"` to disable. A key counts as referenced if it appears as the first argument of `String:translate("…")` / `String:translate('…')` in any indexed `.mez`, or as the value of label-like XML attributes (`label`, `title`, `heading`, `tooltip`, `subject`, `body`, `value`, etc.) in indexed `.vxml` (comments stripped). Keys used only in SQL, Jasper, JSON, or other non-indexed files may false-positive as unused.
- **Unused / unreferenced models** (`helium-dsl-unused`): `diagnostics.unused.models` — default **Info**, `"None"` to disable. Applies to **persistent** objects only: a model counts as referenced when its type name appears in indexed `.mez` type positions (attributes, relationships, unit variables/params/locals/returns, `typeReferences`, etc.). Non-persistent `object` types are not reported.
- Open `.lang` documents are validated without running the DSL parser; Problems show only these unused-entry diagnostics (after `isIndexingComplete`).
- Dev check: `node --import=tsx helium-dsl-language-server/scripts/validate-lang-unused.ts <dsl-root>`.
- When reporting unused functions on a unit, the index also **re-parses matching `.vxml` from disk** for that `unit="..."` so MCP/validate stays aligned with views even if the in-memory VXML map was stale or a file failed to index earlier.

## Lint rule expectations

- Keep `no-var-in-else` detection robust across formatting styles.
- Keep `forbidden-operators` performant on large files and multiline string blocks.
